      "claim": "β-CIT, a close 3β-aryltropane analogue of cocaine, showed 5.5% of injected radioactivity in cynomolgus monkey brain after IV injection, directly demonstrating high BBB permeability for this scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "WIN 35,428, a 3β-(4-fluorophenyl)tropane close analogue, was confirmed by in vivo/ex vivo autoradiography in monkeys to pass the blood-brain barrier and label dopamine transporters.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "PE2I, another cocaine-like tropane, showed 6 ± 1.4% of radioactivity in human brain after intravenous injection, demonstrating meaningful CNS access in humans.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "IPCIT, an isopropyl ester tropane analogue, reached 9.2% of injected dose in baboon brain after IV administration, supporting high brain uptake for this compound class.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Cocaine is the parent compound of a tropane series with repeated direct demonstrations of high brain uptake after systemic administration across species, including primates and humans. The closest analogues share the same tropane core and key substituents, so the measured BBB permeability transfers strongly to the query. Prediction: pass.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct quantitative brain exposure measurement for cocaine itself in the provided records; however, the consistent direct brain-uptake data from close structural analogues and cocaine's established CNS pharmacology make the prediction robust."
